00:22:35.986 --> 00:22:40.470
going to use ML to predict
outcomes for safety,

00:22:40.470 --> 00:22:41.490
based on weather.

00:22:41.490 --> 00:22:43.700
So I'll get to it, and I'll show
you how that all fits together

00:22:43.700 --> 00:22:44.100
and how that works.

00:22:44.100 --> 00:22:44.560
SUDHIR HASBE: Great, Neil.

00:22:44.560 --> 00:22:46.740
And while you are
getting set up on that,

00:22:46.740 --> 00:22:49.170
the key thing is-- there's
another key thing we'll

00:22:49.170 --> 00:22:54.510
be launching today
is it's GIS alpha.

00:22:54.510 --> 00:22:56.550
So BigQuery will
natively support

00:22:56.550 --> 00:22:58.920
GIS capabilities,
like GIS data types,

00:22:58.920 --> 00:23:00.630
within the data warehouse.

00:23:00.630 --> 00:23:02.350
We'll talk more
about it a bit later.

00:23:02.350 --> 00:23:04.024
And there's a detail
session at 3:15

00:23:04.024 --> 00:23:06.690
that we are going to talk about,
but I will hand it over to Neil

00:23:06.690 --> 00:23:08.704
to talk more about the demo.

00:23:08.704 --> 00:23:09.620
NEIL CAWSE: OK, super.

00:23:09.620 --> 00:23:10.710
So we'll get the demo up.

00:23:10.710 --> 00:23:12.120
All right, we're up.

00:23:12.120 --> 00:23:15.182
So what you're seeing over here
is a view inside our product.

00:23:15.182 --> 00:23:16.890
As I mentioned before,
this is an add-in.

00:23:16.890 --> 00:23:18.515
This is one of hundreds
of add-ins that

00:23:18.515 --> 00:23:19.810
are available in the product.

00:23:19.810 --> 00:23:22.410
This is one really cool
one where we're leveraging

00:23:22.410 --> 00:23:24.500
Google ML and Google GIS--

00:23:24.500 --> 00:23:27.680
the BigQuery's GIS features that
have just been announced here

00:23:27.680 --> 00:23:28.845
that we just talked about--

00:23:28.845 --> 00:23:30.720
in order to get some
really interesting data.

00:23:30.720 --> 00:23:32.220
And this is just
starting to scratch

00:23:32.220 --> 00:23:33.960
the surface of where
we can go with this,

00:23:33.960 --> 00:23:35.230
and you can understand.

00:23:35.230 --> 00:23:37.320
So what you're seeing
on the left-hand side

00:23:37.320 --> 00:23:40.590
is a view of the dangerous
intersections in the Chicago

00:23:40.590 --> 00:23:42.270
area, so over the
last two weeks.

00:23:42.270 --> 00:23:43.902
And essentially, the
hotspots are areas

00:23:43.902 --> 00:23:44.985
where it's more dangerous.

00:23:44.985 --> 00:23:46.980
Now you ask, how could
we possibly tell that?

00:23:46.980 --> 00:23:50.820
Well, there's about
100,000 accidents a year

00:23:50.820 --> 00:23:52.380
happening in our
pool of vehicles.

00:23:52.380 --> 00:23:54.213
We know where people
are slamming on brakes,

00:23:54.213 --> 00:23:55.560
so we aggregate that data.

00:23:55.560 --> 00:23:58.860
We can then look at where are
people having these accidents?

00:23:58.860 --> 00:24:01.320
And where are people slamming
on brakes, or dangerous lane

00:24:01.320 --> 00:24:05.050
changes, and
swerving, and whatnot?

00:24:05.050 --> 00:24:07.860
So what happened is the Big
Data team, which are actually

00:24:07.860 --> 00:24:10.200
sitting here today, what they
did is they took the data

00:24:10.200 --> 00:24:12.470
and then they said, let's
use the public dataset that

00:24:12.470 --> 00:24:15.900
was available in Google
BigQuery around weather data.

00:24:15.900 --> 00:24:18.120
And so we know for a
particular date and time,

00:24:18.120 --> 00:24:19.620
for a particular
location, what is

00:24:19.620 --> 00:24:21.090
the weather in that location.

00:24:21.090 --> 00:24:23.100
And they used 250
different metrics

00:24:23.100 --> 00:24:25.890
to analyze and compute,
what can we tell about how

00:24:25.890 --> 00:24:28.724
weather impacts safety?

00:24:28.724 --> 00:24:30.390
And so they ran this
experiment, and I'm

00:24:30.390 --> 00:24:32.015
going to show you
some results of that.

00:24:32.015 --> 00:24:34.860
So let's say we drop the
temperature down to around

00:24:34.860 --> 00:24:38.820
freezing, and let's do snow.

00:24:38.820 --> 00:24:42.300
And I'm going to run the
predictive analysis now live.

00:24:42.300 --> 00:24:44.490
And then what we see is
actually really interesting.

00:24:44.490 --> 00:24:45.870
Some of the areas
that were dangerous

00:24:45.870 --> 00:24:47.520
before are still
dangerous, but there's

00:24:47.520 --> 00:24:49.560
been a big change
in the pattern.

00:24:49.560 --> 00:24:52.210
And so we are seeing things
look remarkably different.

00:24:52.210 --> 00:24:53.760
And if we zoom into
areas now, now we

00:24:53.760 --> 00:24:55.800
can start seeing,
well, where are

00:24:55.800 --> 00:24:57.512
those dangerous intersections?

00:24:57.512 --> 00:24:59.220
Let's just take one
little area over here

00:24:59.220 --> 00:25:00.900
where I'm going to zoom in to.

00:25:00.900 --> 00:25:04.200
And we'll find that
wherever it snows,

00:25:04.200 --> 00:25:07.641
we seem to have a dangerous
area near a school.

00:25:07.641 --> 00:25:09.640
And so we might consider
what is happening here.

00:25:09.640 --> 00:25:12.200
Maybe the parents are
waiting across the road

00:25:12.200 --> 00:25:13.376
to pick up the kids.

00:25:13.376 --> 00:25:14.750
And it's snowing,
and so the kids

00:25:14.750 --> 00:25:16.240
are running across the road.

00:25:16.240 --> 00:25:17.710
And so you get the circumstance.

00:25:17.710 --> 00:25:19.850
Or perhaps vehicles
break down there.

00:25:19.850 --> 00:25:24.500
But the point is, by leveraging
ML, by leveraging this data,

00:25:24.500 --> 00:25:28.080
cities can now look at
what the infrastructure is

00:25:28.080 --> 00:25:30.080
and change the way the
roads are set up in order

00:25:30.080 --> 00:25:31.850
to keep everybody safer.

00:25:31.850 --> 00:25:34.670
And this is really just starting
to scratch the surface of what

00:25:34.670 --> 00:25:36.260
you can do when
you leverage such

00:25:36.260 --> 00:25:38.510
a powerful tool, like Google
BigQuery and Google ML.

00:25:38.510 --> 00:25:39.760
SUDHIR HASBE: Thank you, Neil.

00:25:39.760 --> 00:25:40.549
This is awesome.

00:25:40.549 --> 00:25:41.090
Thanks a lot.

00:25:41.090 --> 00:25:41.870
NEIL CAWSE: Thank you.

00:25:41.870 --> 00:25:43.328
SUDHIR HASBE: The
key thing is just

00:25:43.328 --> 00:25:45.960
making the city smarter and
just having that kind of impact.

00:25:45.960 --> 00:25:48.530
And you can actually do model
generation and prediction

00:25:48.530 --> 00:25:51.230
so fast, it's just going to
expedite the whole solution

00:25:51.230 --> 00:25:51.730
creation.

00:25:51.730 --> 00:25:52.460
NEIL CAWSE: Absolutely.

00:25:52.460 --> 00:25:54.376
One of the key things
was how quickly our team

00:25:54.376 --> 00:25:55.700
was able to put this together.

00:25:55.700 --> 00:25:57.584
There is no coding involved.

00:25:57.584 --> 00:25:58.500
There's no Kubernetes.

00:25:58.500 --> 00:26:00.620
There's no spinning of
magnitudes of servers.

00:26:00.620 --> 00:26:03.680
SUDHIR HASBE: We love Kubernetes
too, but we have SQL people.

00:26:03.680 --> 00:26:04.549
We love SQL, so.

00:26:04.549 --> 00:26:05.465
NEIL CAWSE: All right.

00:26:05.465 --> 00:26:06.170
Thank you.

00:26:06.170 --> 00:26:06.380
SUDHIR HASBE: Thanks, Neil.

00:26:06.380 --> 00:26:07.383
Thanks a lot.

