00:28:36.640 --> 00:28:39.040
And with availability
of GIS features,

00:28:39.040 --> 00:28:43.900
you can do that kind of queries
directly inside the query now.

00:28:43.900 --> 00:28:46.540
We have some new connectors
that are going live.

00:28:46.540 --> 00:28:48.430
One of the other key
things we are launching

00:28:48.430 --> 00:28:52.660
is our new BigQuery
UI, which will give you

00:28:52.660 --> 00:28:55.390
the capabilities
is it looks better,

00:28:55.390 --> 00:28:57.340
and it also has some
one-click experiences

00:28:57.340 --> 00:29:00.370
to go into Data Studio
and do visualization.

00:29:00.370 --> 00:29:02.200
And then we'll
quickly take a look

00:29:02.200 --> 00:29:04.670
at Google Sheets integration
that's available.

00:29:04.670 --> 00:29:05.890
So this is an example.

00:29:05.890 --> 00:29:09.970
Along with the GIS capability
of the core data types

00:29:09.970 --> 00:29:11.890
and their ability
to query, we also

00:29:11.890 --> 00:29:14.110
have a visual tool
that we are launching,

00:29:14.110 --> 00:29:17.014
which allows you to go ahead
and visually fire a query

00:29:17.014 --> 00:29:18.430
and look at the
points on the map.

00:29:18.430 --> 00:29:21.214
Because if you're doing
a query around, hey,

00:29:21.214 --> 00:29:23.380
show me all the points that
are in a two-mile radius

00:29:23.380 --> 00:29:25.546
of another point, how are
you going to visualize it?

00:29:25.546 --> 00:29:28.300
It's really difficult. So we
worked with our Earth Engine

00:29:28.300 --> 00:29:30.577
team at Google and
have this visual tool

00:29:30.577 --> 00:29:32.660
that gives you the ability
to visualize that data.

00:29:32.660 --> 00:29:35.290
So please take a look at that.

00:29:35.290 --> 00:29:39.010
Again with Sheets, as I
said, a lot of our customers

00:29:39.010 --> 00:29:43.060
use Sheets for analysis
and move data into it.

00:29:43.060 --> 00:29:46.300
Now with Google Sheets, you
have a connector for BigQuery.

00:29:46.300 --> 00:29:48.310
From within there,
you can just click,

00:29:48.310 --> 00:29:49.780
connect to your
BigQuery instance,

00:29:49.780 --> 00:29:52.090
pull data in, and
start analyzing that,

00:29:52.090 --> 00:29:53.930
visualizing it out-of-the-box.

00:29:53.930 --> 00:29:55.900
So one of the other
key capabilities--

00:29:55.900 --> 00:29:59.500
making it easy to go do
that analysis, connect

00:29:59.500 --> 00:30:00.670
to the datasets, and all.

00:30:00.670 --> 00:30:04.180
So that's been one of our
big themes for this year.

00:30:04.180 --> 00:30:06.130
So that's BigQuery--
how do you make

00:30:06.130 --> 00:30:09.070
it easy to go ahead and
analyze data on BigQuery?

00:30:09.070 --> 00:30:12.059
Streaming analytics-- I
touched upon it earlier.

00:30:12.059 --> 00:30:13.600
We have a whole
portfolio of products

00:30:13.600 --> 00:30:16.099
that allow you to do that, like
you can do millions of event

00:30:16.099 --> 00:30:18.680
collection from using Pub/Sub.

00:30:18.680 --> 00:30:21.280
Dataflow allows you to do
large scale data processing.

00:30:21.280 --> 00:30:24.910
You can use Cloud ML or BigQuery
to go ahead and do analysis

00:30:24.910 --> 00:30:27.310
on top of that data.

00:30:27.310 --> 00:30:29.830
Brightcove is one of the
best examples of this.

00:30:29.830 --> 00:30:35.480
They literally collect 8,500
years of video per month.

00:30:35.480 --> 00:30:38.800
That's seven billion events
a day is what they collect.

00:30:38.800 --> 00:30:41.560
And they use
Dataflow plus Pub/Sub

00:30:41.560 --> 00:30:44.740
to go ahead and analyze
those videos and leverage

00:30:44.740 --> 00:30:46.660
some great insights from it.

00:30:46.660 --> 00:30:48.560
But it's not just
the Brightcove.

00:30:48.560 --> 00:30:52.000
Traveloka uses it for
e-commerce, clickstream

00:30:52.000 --> 00:30:53.895
collection, and analyzing that.

00:30:53.895 --> 00:30:56.020
Qubit is another example
where, in retail, they are

00:30:56.020 --> 00:30:59.200
doing point-of-sale analysis.

00:30:59.200 --> 00:31:02.810
Amazing scenarios with
Nintendo in in-game analysis,

00:31:02.810 --> 00:31:05.560
in-game utilization
of consumables.

00:31:05.560 --> 00:31:07.240
And then also Nest for IoT data.

00:31:07.240 --> 00:31:11.180
So any kind of large scale-event
collection processing

00:31:11.180 --> 00:31:15.800
analytics, you can use
Pub/Sub, Dataflow for that.

00:31:15.800 --> 00:31:19.270
We are actually announcing a
few enhancements in that space.

00:31:19.270 --> 00:31:22.280
One of the big things that
we are doing is Python.

00:31:22.280 --> 00:31:26.180
Python is one of the fastest
growing languages in GitHub,

00:31:26.180 --> 00:31:28.370
if you just look at all
of the comments and all.

00:31:28.370 --> 00:31:31.580
And we wanted to make it easy
for our Python developers

00:31:31.580 --> 00:31:32.900
to do streaming.

00:31:32.900 --> 00:31:35.840
So now, we are going to enable
Python streaming capability

00:31:35.840 --> 00:31:39.560
with Beam, so customers can
actually build scalable data

00:31:39.560 --> 00:31:41.510
pipelines using Python.

00:31:41.510 --> 00:31:43.130
So that's going in beta now.

00:31:43.130 --> 00:31:45.960
So customers can use that.

00:31:45.960 --> 00:31:49.460
We also have Dataflow streaming
and shuffle capability.

00:31:49.460 --> 00:31:52.160
It will help you do large-scale
data processing easily.

00:31:52.160 --> 00:31:54.204
Auto-scaling capabilities
will come with it.

00:31:54.204 --> 00:31:56.120
There are detailed
deep-dive sessions on these

00:31:56.120 --> 00:31:59.270
that you should check
out if you're interested.

00:31:59.270 --> 00:32:01.790
One of the other
things we have done

00:32:01.790 --> 00:32:05.930
is we have actually enhanced
the performance on and made

00:32:05.930 --> 00:32:09.020
our libraries much more
efficient for Pub/Sub

00:32:09.020 --> 00:32:12.120
in seven different
languages that you can use.

00:32:12.120 --> 00:32:14.180
But in addition to
that, we have a lot

00:32:14.180 --> 00:32:16.180
of customers who love Kafka.

00:32:16.180 --> 00:32:18.590
They're like, hey,
I use Kafka already.

00:32:18.590 --> 00:32:20.990
I want to continue
using it on GCP.

00:32:20.990 --> 00:32:22.500
What are my options?

00:32:22.500 --> 00:32:25.250
So historically, you could just
go ahead and deploy it yourself

00:32:25.250 --> 00:32:26.390
and manage it.

00:32:26.390 --> 00:32:28.910
But what we have now
is, with Confluent,

00:32:28.910 --> 00:32:31.157
we have a managed Kafka
solution that's available.

00:32:31.157 --> 00:32:32.990
So if you want to go
ahead and use a managed

00:32:32.990 --> 00:32:36.440
service for Kafka, you can just
use Confluent Kafka on GCP.

00:32:36.440 --> 00:32:39.410
And that's one of
our strategies is

00:32:39.410 --> 00:32:40.940
to work with our
partners to provide

00:32:40.940 --> 00:32:44.800
these end-to-end solutions that
you can leverage as a customer.

00:32:44.800 --> 00:32:49.770
So it's already available
that you can use.

00:32:49.770 --> 00:32:52.790
One of the other things,
which is core to our strategy

00:32:52.790 --> 00:32:58.160
as well as core belief,
is this open source

00:32:58.160 --> 00:32:59.930
and being an open cloud.

00:32:59.930 --> 00:33:02.550
And we fundamentally look
at the things from Istio

00:33:02.550 --> 00:33:04.820
to Kubernetes that
we're are investing in.

00:33:04.820 --> 00:33:06.320
On our side in the
big-data world,

00:33:06.320 --> 00:33:09.642
we are investing a lot in
open-source technologes.

00:33:09.642 --> 00:33:11.600
Like if you look at just
like big data roadmap,

00:33:11.600 --> 00:33:14.450
last 15 years, the
amount of innovation

00:33:14.450 --> 00:33:17.290
that Google has driven
and made available--

00:33:17.290 --> 00:33:19.790
before Google Cloud, we used
to make this available as paper

00:33:19.790 --> 00:33:22.850
so that the industry could learn
from all the research that we

00:33:22.850 --> 00:33:25.040
had done, everything
from Dremel paper,

00:33:25.040 --> 00:33:28.760
to MapReduce, to GFS,
like all different papers.

00:33:28.760 --> 00:33:31.070
And then we also are building
a lot of these products

00:33:31.070 --> 00:33:34.590
based on these technologies.

00:33:34.590 --> 00:33:37.910
There are two key product areas
that we have been investing in

00:33:37.910 --> 00:33:39.570
on open-source side.

00:33:39.570 --> 00:33:40.640
One is Dataproc.

00:33:40.640 --> 00:33:43.850
It's managed Hadoop
and Spark capability,

00:33:43.850 --> 00:33:45.480
as well as Composer.

00:33:45.480 --> 00:33:47.790
Composer is fascinating.

00:33:47.790 --> 00:33:51.050
When it was in private alpha,
we had more than 1,000 customers

00:33:51.050 --> 00:33:52.040
using it.

00:33:52.040 --> 00:33:54.030
I have no idea how do
you keep it private,

00:33:54.030 --> 00:33:56.030
and then they have that
many customers using it.

00:33:56.030 --> 00:33:58.250
So it just took off.

00:33:58.250 --> 00:34:00.680
It's based on Airflow,
Apache Airflow,

00:34:00.680 --> 00:34:03.450
and it was just basically
all the customers loved it.

00:34:03.450 --> 00:34:07.580
And we started seeing a
tremendous adoption of it.

00:34:07.580 --> 00:34:10.639
So we are announcing
the GA for Composer now.

00:34:10.639 --> 00:34:11.929
So it's already available.

00:34:11.929 --> 00:34:13.580
You should be able to use it.

00:34:13.580 --> 00:34:17.900
Major enhancements in
our Dataproc side--

00:34:17.900 --> 00:34:20.330
auto-scaling and
custom packages.

00:34:20.330 --> 00:34:22.400
Custom packages allow
you, with a few clicks,

00:34:22.400 --> 00:34:24.650
to pick our top-level
Apache projects

00:34:24.650 --> 00:34:27.199
that you want to go ahead
and deploy now in Dataproc.

00:34:27.199 --> 00:34:28.909
That's interesting.

00:34:28.909 --> 00:34:31.730
And auto-scaling based
on your resource needs

00:34:31.730 --> 00:34:34.420
automatically will scale
your Hadoop clusters, Hadoop

00:34:34.420 --> 00:34:37.440
and Spark clusters,
on your behalf.

00:34:37.440 --> 00:34:39.530
And then, of course, we
announced a few weeks back

00:34:39.530 --> 00:34:42.770
that [? Toddenworks ?] now
supports their infrastructure

00:34:42.770 --> 00:34:44.340
and GCP natively.

00:34:44.340 --> 00:34:47.810
So you can use HDP or
HDF directly on GCP.

00:34:47.810 --> 00:34:52.070
With that, let me call upon
Michael from Blue Apron

00:34:52.070 --> 00:34:57.023
to talk about how
they're using GCP.

00:34:57.023 --> 00:34:57.522
[APPLAUSE]

00:34:57.522 --> 00:34:58.866
Michael.

00:34:58.866 --> 00:34:59.770
Welcome.

00:34:59.770 --> 00:34:59.840
MICHAEL COLLIS: Hi Sudhir.

00:34:59.840 --> 00:35:00.502
How are you doing?

00:35:00.502 --> 00:35:00.954
SUDHIR HASBE: Good.

00:35:00.954 --> 00:35:01.406
MICHAEL COLLIS: Good to be here.

00:35:01.406 --> 00:35:02.960
SUDHIR HASBE: Can you do
a quick intro of yourself,

00:35:02.960 --> 00:35:04.620
as well as the
company, your role?

00:35:04.620 --> 00:35:05.495
MICHAEL COLLIS: Sure.

00:35:05.495 --> 00:35:06.000
Absolutely.

00:35:06.000 --> 00:35:07.110
Hey, everyone.

00:35:07.110 --> 00:35:09.920
Hope you're enjoying the
second day of Next, like I am.

00:35:09.920 --> 00:35:15.632
So Blue Apron was founded
six years ago with a--

00:35:15.632 --> 00:35:18.080
well, with a modest goal.

00:35:18.080 --> 00:35:20.810
And that goal was to
re-envision how the food system

00:35:20.810 --> 00:35:22.860
worked in this country.

00:35:22.860 --> 00:35:25.400
And so while we've made
some good progress,

00:35:25.400 --> 00:35:28.580
that is an audacious goal as
visions are supposed to be.

00:35:28.580 --> 00:35:30.500
And we thought we can
get at this vision

00:35:30.500 --> 00:35:35.390
by making home cooking
more accessible, easier,

00:35:35.390 --> 00:35:37.850
more affordable for more
people in this country.

00:35:37.850 --> 00:35:40.680
And in doing so, we
could go out there,

00:35:40.680 --> 00:35:42.980
work with farmers,
producers, and make sure

00:35:42.980 --> 00:35:47.750
that we were investing in
sustainable agriculture,

00:35:47.750 --> 00:35:51.770
humane ways to raise livestock,
all these different things.

00:35:51.770 --> 00:35:56.870
So basically, what
we do is we send out

00:35:56.870 --> 00:36:02.330
proportioned, seasonal
ingredients to you in a box

00:36:02.330 --> 00:36:04.130
with a recipe to make those.

00:36:04.130 --> 00:36:07.040
And we are around millions
of dinner tables in the US

00:36:07.040 --> 00:36:10.880
every single night,
which is a privilege.

00:36:10.880 --> 00:36:13.520
SUDHIR HASBE: I'm one of
them, so I love Blue Apron.

00:36:13.520 --> 00:36:14.390
MICHAEL COLLIS: OK.

00:36:14.390 --> 00:36:18.570
SUDHIR HASBE: So how is data
analytics used at Blue Apron?

00:36:18.570 --> 00:36:22.090
MICHAEL COLLIS: So one of
the greatest privileges

00:36:22.090 --> 00:36:26.230
about working in food, I
think, that I've learned

00:36:26.230 --> 00:36:30.370
is that people always want
to tell you what they think.

00:36:30.370 --> 00:36:32.890
We don't have to really go
out and solicit much customer

00:36:32.890 --> 00:36:34.966
feedback.

00:36:34.966 --> 00:36:37.380
[LAUGHTER]

00:36:37.380 --> 00:36:38.050
No.

00:36:38.050 --> 00:36:40.050
As I said, you're around
people's dinner tables.

00:36:40.050 --> 00:36:41.700
It's a very personal
moment, right?

00:36:41.700 --> 00:36:43.200
And it's very intimate.

00:36:43.200 --> 00:36:46.910
And basically, we have a
responsibility to listen.

00:36:46.910 --> 00:36:49.440
And as I said,
people will show us,

00:36:49.440 --> 00:36:52.770
they'll tell us what they
want in their recipes.

00:36:52.770 --> 00:36:55.680
We were joking before
that all the recipes have

00:36:55.680 --> 00:36:57.270
kale in them in the summer.

00:36:57.270 --> 00:36:58.590
Don't ask me to fix that.

00:36:58.590 --> 00:37:01.050
I can't fix that.

00:37:01.050 --> 00:37:05.640
So data is a really
core part of how

00:37:05.640 --> 00:37:08.890
we make our business decisions.

00:37:08.890 --> 00:37:10.707
And that's not
immediately obvious,

00:37:10.707 --> 00:37:11.790
if you look at what we do.

00:37:11.790 --> 00:37:13.120
You think, oh, you
ship a box of food.

00:37:13.120 --> 00:37:13.620
OK.

00:37:13.620 --> 00:37:16.050
So that's great.

00:37:16.050 --> 00:37:20.420
But actually, we are looking
at the customer lifecycle

00:37:20.420 --> 00:37:22.550
at every stage, and
we are ingesting data

00:37:22.550 --> 00:37:28.100
about what you like, what
recipes appeal to you,

00:37:28.100 --> 00:37:30.410
what photos appeal to you,
what titles appeal to you.

00:37:30.410 --> 00:37:35.190
And we're building up a
profile of what you like.

00:37:35.190 --> 00:37:38.019
And as I said, people
tell us how they feel.

00:37:38.019 --> 00:37:40.310
If you've ever written a
comment on one of our recipes,

00:37:40.310 --> 00:37:42.600
know that a human
being has read it.

00:37:42.600 --> 00:37:44.110
SUDHIR HASBE: That's awesome.

00:37:44.110 --> 00:37:46.050
MICHAEL COLLIS: But we
can do better, right?

00:37:46.050 --> 00:37:48.130
And what we think
is we can build

00:37:48.130 --> 00:37:50.890
a virtuous cycle around
what we're doing here

00:37:50.890 --> 00:37:52.660
and the vision with data.

00:37:52.660 --> 00:37:57.540
And the way that we think
about doing that is--

00:37:57.540 --> 00:37:59.966
just if we use an
example of something

00:37:59.966 --> 00:38:01.340
that my team does
a lot of, which

00:38:01.340 --> 00:38:04.700
is recipe recommendations,
obviously-- helping people

00:38:04.700 --> 00:38:06.590
make sure we put the
right recipe in the box

00:38:06.590 --> 00:38:08.950
that you'll like, obviously.

00:38:08.950 --> 00:38:11.836
So if we have better
recommendations,

00:38:11.836 --> 00:38:12.960
we have better forecasting.

00:38:12.960 --> 00:38:14.160
We have better purchasing.

00:38:14.160 --> 00:38:17.020
We are going out and sourcing
the right ingredients,

00:38:17.020 --> 00:38:21.330
and the right proteins, and
the right dry goods that

00:38:21.330 --> 00:38:22.790
meet our needs.

00:38:22.790 --> 00:38:24.500
That is reducing food waste.

00:38:24.500 --> 00:38:30.380
It is cutting out another
middleman in the step, right?

00:38:30.380 --> 00:38:31.730
The supermarket.

00:38:31.730 --> 00:38:37.190
And if we get better
at that, we end up

00:38:37.190 --> 00:38:42.350
saving thousands and thousands
of tons of wasted food, right?

00:38:42.350 --> 00:38:46.560
So every small change is really
important for us in that.

00:38:46.560 --> 00:38:50.534
And at scale, it makes
a huge difference.

00:38:50.534 --> 00:38:52.700
SUDHIR HASBE: And tell me
more about your philosophy

00:38:52.700 --> 00:38:54.710
around open-source
software, and how

00:38:54.710 --> 00:38:57.230
you use it, and stuff like
that within the organization.

00:38:57.230 --> 00:38:58.400
MICHAEL COLLIS: Right.

00:38:58.400 --> 00:39:01.850
So we're on the record
as using a laundry

00:39:01.850 --> 00:39:04.040
list of GCP services--

00:39:04.040 --> 00:39:07.670
you know, Iowa Enterprise
Data Warehouse is, BigQuery.

00:39:07.670 --> 00:39:12.650
We use Dataflow for our
streaming processing.

00:39:12.650 --> 00:39:16.230
We use Dataproc for our
batch machine learning.

00:39:16.230 --> 00:39:19.977
We use GCS for our data lake,
for our prepared features,

00:39:19.977 --> 00:39:21.560
our trained models,
all of this stuff.

00:39:24.180 --> 00:39:27.350
But a lot of that orchestration,
we use Airflow for.

00:39:27.350 --> 00:39:31.110
We have been using Airflow
from, more or less,

00:39:31.110 --> 00:39:33.630
day one that data engineering
existed at Blue Apron.

00:39:33.630 --> 00:39:35.400
And it's incredibly
important for us

00:39:35.400 --> 00:39:37.710
because it helps us
ingest information

00:39:37.710 --> 00:39:39.000
from outside sources.

00:39:39.000 --> 00:39:42.580
It helps us run
batch ETL processes.

00:39:42.580 --> 00:39:44.640
It helps us run our
batch machine learning

00:39:44.640 --> 00:39:48.210
models, all of that stuff.

00:39:48.210 --> 00:39:51.120
And it's actually a key
piece of how we end up

00:39:51.120 --> 00:39:53.760
actually serving our batch
machine learning predictions

00:39:53.760 --> 00:39:55.600
as well.

00:39:55.600 --> 00:40:00.000
We use Airflow to compute
122 million recommendations

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:00.900
every day.

00:40:00.900 --> 00:40:05.040
And we load those up into
a little level DB artifact

00:40:05.040 --> 00:40:09.980
that we serve in memory from
our services, which is great,

00:40:09.980 --> 00:40:11.570
because it means
we can serve 122

00:40:11.570 --> 00:40:16.140
million recommendations daily
with about 15-microsecond

00:40:16.140 --> 00:40:16.640
latency.

00:40:16.640 --> 00:40:17.580
That's pretty good.

00:40:17.580 --> 00:40:17.930
SUDHIR HASBE: Wow.

00:40:17.930 --> 00:40:18.860
MICHAEL COLLIS: We
can work with that.

00:40:18.860 --> 00:40:20.090
SUDHIR HASBE: That's awesome.

00:40:20.090 --> 00:40:20.360
MICHAEL COLLIS: Right.

00:40:20.360 --> 00:40:22.276
But open source is a
huge part of that, right?

00:40:22.276 --> 00:40:24.339
We got burned early on by--

00:40:24.339 --> 00:40:26.630
I think this story should be
familiar to everyone who's

00:40:26.630 --> 00:40:28.310
worked as a startup, maybe.

00:40:28.310 --> 00:40:33.230
We got burned early on by vendor
lock-in on certain clouds.

00:40:33.230 --> 00:40:37.650
And we've been committed to
open source in the beginning.

00:40:37.650 --> 00:40:40.200
But that really made
us realize, oh, we

00:40:40.200 --> 00:40:41.970
have to take open
source seriously

00:40:41.970 --> 00:40:46.980
as an engineering organization
and not end up in that position

00:40:46.980 --> 00:40:48.632
again.

00:40:48.632 --> 00:40:50.340
We're not a big
engineering organization.

00:40:50.340 --> 00:40:52.870
Data engineering, for
us, we're only 15 people.

00:40:52.870 --> 00:40:56.300
We've got to work on what we
have competitive advantages in,

00:40:56.300 --> 00:40:59.040
and that is not running Airflow.

00:40:59.040 --> 00:41:02.250
Our Data Operations team managed
to do the most recent Airflow

00:41:02.250 --> 00:41:05.820
1.9 update on our cluster.

00:41:05.820 --> 00:41:06.320
Yeah.

00:41:06.320 --> 00:41:13.060
Well, they do not
sleep well that week.

00:41:13.060 --> 00:41:16.492
So we don't want
to get locked in.

00:41:16.492 --> 00:41:18.450
And we want to write it
once, run it everywhere

00:41:18.450 --> 00:41:19.650
in our hybrid cloud.

00:41:19.650 --> 00:41:23.880
And when Google
is saying, we are

00:41:23.880 --> 00:41:26.550
committed to an open cloud,
that's very important for us.

00:41:26.550 --> 00:41:30.900
And that's very
important because you

00:41:30.900 --> 00:41:36.870
can compete for our business
on any other dimension,

00:41:36.870 --> 00:41:39.810
but it's not you're
locked into our product.

00:41:39.810 --> 00:41:43.680
And that matters a lot, like
that's a good signal to us.

00:41:47.580 --> 00:41:50.090
Beam, Spark, TensorFlow--
these are all things that we

00:41:50.090 --> 00:41:51.090
have big investments in.

00:41:51.090 --> 00:41:56.730
And if it's open-source, we
can move it anywhere we like,

00:41:56.730 --> 00:41:57.402
and we're not--

00:41:57.402 --> 00:41:59.860
SUDHIR HASBE: We hope you'll
never move them, but I get it.

00:41:59.860 --> 00:42:02.100
You have an option of moving
them whenever you want.

00:42:02.100 --> 00:42:03.458
MICHAEL COLLIS: I could.

00:42:03.458 --> 00:42:04.910
[LAUGHTER]

00:42:04.910 --> 00:42:05.910
SUDHIR HASBE: Perfect.

00:42:05.910 --> 00:42:06.410
Thanks.

00:42:06.410 --> 00:42:07.500
Thanks a lot, Michael.

00:42:07.500 --> 00:42:09.294
Any other key metrics
that you have seen

00:42:09.294 --> 00:42:10.710
or business results
you would like

00:42:10.710 --> 00:42:12.844
to share before we wrap up?

00:42:12.844 --> 00:42:15.510
MICHAEL COLLIS: You can't ask me
that on the week of an earnings

00:42:15.510 --> 00:42:16.620
release.

00:42:16.620 --> 00:42:20.160
But no, I mean,
basically, we have

00:42:20.160 --> 00:42:25.440
seen a huge amount of uptick
in engagement with our product.

00:42:25.440 --> 00:42:29.157
And when we give customers
more ways to give us feedback,

00:42:29.157 --> 00:42:30.240
we get even more feedback.

00:42:30.240 --> 00:42:33.390
So it is a really
virtuous cycle there.

00:42:33.390 --> 00:42:35.280
And we're also
using those insights

00:42:35.280 --> 00:42:38.550
to help our culinary team and
our amazing chefs basically

00:42:38.550 --> 00:42:39.880
plan recipes better.

00:42:39.880 --> 00:42:42.410
So that's a new,
exciting frontier for us.

00:42:42.410 --> 00:42:45.630
It's using AI to actually
provide feedback from what we

00:42:45.630 --> 00:42:48.180
know our customers will like,
so that on the menu are more

00:42:48.180 --> 00:42:49.710
things that---

00:42:49.710 --> 00:42:52.290
you know, there's something
for everyone and things

00:42:52.290 --> 00:42:54.374
that people are going
to love that much more.

00:42:54.374 --> 00:42:55.290
SUDHIR HASBE: Awesome.

00:42:55.290 --> 00:42:55.590
Thank you.

00:42:55.590 --> 00:42:56.170
Thanks, Michael.

00:42:56.170 --> 00:42:56.460
MICHAEL COLLIS: Right.

00:42:56.460 --> 00:42:56.970
Thanks.

00:42:56.970 --> 00:42:57.970
SUDHIR HASBE: Thank you.

00:42:57.970 --> 00:43:00.070
[APPLAUSE]

00:43:00.070 --> 00:43:04.230
So as you saw, when I talk to
customers across the board,

00:43:04.230 --> 00:43:08.340
this whole thing is on open
cloud actually resonates a lot,

00:43:08.340 --> 00:43:10.620
especially keeping the
expertise customers

00:43:10.620 --> 00:43:14.370
have in Spark, Hadoop, as well
as with Beam what we have done,

00:43:14.370 --> 00:43:17.336
and other areas.

00:43:17.336 --> 00:43:19.210
The fourth topic I want
to talk about quickly

00:43:19.210 --> 00:43:21.520
was visualizing and
activating your data.

00:43:21.520 --> 00:43:23.760
The key thing is
self-service BI is

00:43:23.760 --> 00:43:26.520
one of the priorities in
various organizations.

00:43:26.520 --> 00:43:28.380
How do you explore
your data yourself,

00:43:28.380 --> 00:43:32.100
like enable your users to
go out and explore the data,

00:43:32.100 --> 00:43:35.810
as well as do collaborative
data-driven decision making,

00:43:35.810 --> 00:43:38.130
are the topics that come
up in every conversation

00:43:38.130 --> 00:43:39.990
I have with customers.

00:43:39.990 --> 00:43:43.030
So one of the things-- if
you haven't used Data Studio,

00:43:43.030 --> 00:43:46.260
it's our BI tool that's
available, highly

00:43:46.260 --> 00:43:46.950
collaborative.

00:43:46.950 --> 00:43:50.040
It's basically built
around collaboration.

00:43:50.040 --> 00:43:54.210
The key thing is with the
new BigQuery UI capability

00:43:54.210 --> 00:43:56.640
that I announced, if
you use the new UI,

00:43:56.640 --> 00:43:59.580
you can literally do a
one-click from your query.

00:43:59.580 --> 00:44:02.250
Click once, and directly
do a visualization and data

00:44:02.250 --> 00:44:02.950
exploration.

00:44:02.950 --> 00:44:05.550
So you can go explore
what dataset it is.

00:44:05.550 --> 00:44:07.560
You can blend that data
with other sources,

00:44:07.560 --> 00:44:11.430
like AdWords or something,
and pull that data in.

00:44:11.430 --> 00:44:14.880
And you can actually go ahead
and create a report out of it

00:44:14.880 --> 00:44:15.600
within seconds.

00:44:15.600 --> 00:44:19.000
Like literally, you don't need
a specialist to go do that.

00:44:19.000 --> 00:44:21.210
Additionally, we also
have pre-built templates

00:44:21.210 --> 00:44:22.367
that are available now.

00:44:22.367 --> 00:44:23.700
So you can literally go in and--

00:44:23.700 --> 00:44:26.250
there's actually a template
I found on Cloud Billing.

00:44:26.250 --> 00:44:28.260
So if you want to just
visualize your billing

00:44:28.260 --> 00:44:30.150
on cloud, or Google
Cloud, you can actually

00:44:30.150 --> 00:44:31.191
have a template for that.

00:44:31.191 --> 00:44:33.190
Or you want to analyze
your AdWords stuff,

00:44:33.190 --> 00:44:34.660
you have a template for that.

00:44:34.660 --> 00:44:36.150
So really good capabilities.

00:44:36.150 --> 00:44:39.570
We also have our data
visualization developer preview

00:44:39.570 --> 00:44:40.560
that's available.

00:44:40.560 --> 00:44:43.740
But you can do a
D3-based visualizations,

00:44:43.740 --> 00:44:45.690
create custom visualizations.

00:44:45.690 --> 00:44:48.000
The other area that
we have invested

00:44:48.000 --> 00:44:50.430
with one of our
partners, Trifecta,

00:44:50.430 --> 00:44:52.390
is the data prep solution.

00:44:52.390 --> 00:44:54.000
So a lot of our
customers want to do

00:44:54.000 --> 00:44:58.260
data wrangling, as analysts
want to go do that visually.

00:44:58.260 --> 00:45:01.680
Data Prep actually allows
you to go ahead and visualize

00:45:01.680 --> 00:45:03.600
your data, which
may be in BigQuery,

00:45:03.600 --> 00:45:06.180
figure out what anomalies
are there in the data,

00:45:06.180 --> 00:45:08.730
clean that data up,
and store it back.

00:45:08.730 --> 00:45:10.530
As we are getting
ready for our GA

00:45:10.530 --> 00:45:14.200
with that tool over
the next few months,

00:45:14.200 --> 00:45:16.260
the key thing is we
have focused a lot

00:45:16.260 --> 00:45:18.150
on getting the
feedback from beta,

00:45:18.150 --> 00:45:20.250
and we'll have some key
capabilities available.

00:45:20.250 --> 00:45:22.740
One big area of
enhancements we have done

00:45:22.740 --> 00:45:24.990
is all-around team-based
data wrangling.

00:45:24.990 --> 00:45:29.520
How do you share your recipes,
share and copy your flows?

00:45:29.520 --> 00:45:34.567
How do you go ahead and reuse
your custom sample recipes

00:45:34.567 --> 00:45:35.400
and stuff like that?

00:45:35.400 --> 00:45:37.500
So a lot of focus on that.

00:45:37.500 --> 00:45:39.900
Focus more on productivity,
like how do you

00:45:39.900 --> 00:45:41.670
go ahead and have
quick shortcuts

00:45:41.670 --> 00:45:43.320
to the popular items and all?

00:45:43.320 --> 00:45:46.800
And then we have a completely
new comprehensive design,

00:45:46.800 --> 00:45:49.330
which looks much better
and is way more efficient.

00:45:49.330 --> 00:45:51.690
So that's one of the areas.

00:45:51.690 --> 00:45:55.530
Before I jump into the next
one, so one of the other things.

00:45:55.530 --> 00:46:00.090
Somebody told me a while back,
being good is not enough,

00:46:00.090 --> 00:46:01.620
you should also do good.

00:46:01.620 --> 00:46:04.260
So we have been working
with some nonprofits

00:46:04.260 --> 00:46:08.670
to see how we can help
democratize our analytics

00:46:08.670 --> 00:46:11.580
and machine learning
capabilities

00:46:11.580 --> 00:46:13.470
in the nonprofit community.

